**Rate Limiting:**

To prevent brute-force attacks, we can implement a simple rate limiting mechanism using a counter variable that increments each time the vulnerable page is accessed. We'll also set a maximum number of requests allowed within a certain time frame (e.g., 5 requests per minute).

```sql
CREATE TABLE rate_limit (
    ip_address VARCHAR(45) PRIMARY KEY,
    request_count INT DEFAULT 0,
    last_request TIMESTAMP DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP
);

DELIMITER //
CREATE PROCEDURE check_rate_limit(IN ip_address VARCHAR(45))
BEGIN
    DECLARE current_time TIMESTAMP;
    SET current_time = NOW();
    UPDATE rate_limit
    SET request_count = request_count + 1, 
        last_request = current_time
    WHERE ip_address = :ip_address;

    IF (request_count > 5) THEN
        SELECT 'Rate limit exceeded. Please try again later.' AS message;
        LEAVE;
    END IF;
END//
DELIMITER ;
```

**CAPTCHA:**

To prevent automated attacks, we can generate a CAPTCHA image and require the user to solve it before accessing the vulnerable page.

```sql
CREATE TABLE captcha (
    id INT PRIMARY KEY AUTO_INCREMENT,
    image BLOB,
    solution VARCHAR(45)
);

DELIMITER //
CREATE PROCEDURE generate_captcha()
BEGIN
    DECLARE id INT;
    SET id = 0;

    WHILE (id < 5) DO
        SET @image_data = CONCAT('INSERT INTO captcha (id, image, solution) VALUES (', id, ', MD5(RAND()), MD5(RAND()));');
        PREPARE stmt FROM @image_data;
        EXECUTE stmt;
        DEALLOCATE PREPARE stmt;
        SET id = id + 1;
    END WHILE;
END//
DELIMITER ;
```

